citations.quote:
Let $\lambda\in P^+$ and let $B(\lambda)=\mathcal B(\lambda)$ be the crystal of $V(\lambda)=L(\lambda)$ from [[def-kashiwara-lattices-lambda-and-infinity]], which is a crystal basis by [[thm-grand-loop-existence-of-highest-weight-crystal-bases]]. Then $B(\lambda)$ is a highest weight crystal of highest weight $\lambda$ in the sense of [[def-highest-weight-element-and-connected-highest-weight-crystal]]:

(i) $b_\lambda:=v_\lambda+q\mathcal L(\lambda)$ is a highest weight element of weight $\lambda$: $wt(b_\lambda)=\lambda$ and $\tilde e_ib_\lambda=0$ for every $i\in I$;

(ii) $B(\lambda)=F(b_\lambda)$: every $b\in\mathcal B(\lambda)$ is an iterated lowering image $b=\tilde f_{i_1}\cdots\tilde f_{i_r}v_\lambda$;

(iii) $b_\lambda$ is the only highest weight element of $B(\lambda)$: $\mathcal B(\lambda)_\lambda=\{b_\lambda\}$, and for every $\mu\ne\lambda$ and every nonzero homogeneous $u\in\mathcal L(\lambda)_\mu/q\mathcal L(\lambda)_\mu$ there is $i\in I$ with $\tilde e_iu\ne0$.

No choice principle is used.

derivations.claim:
The raw extension map on divided words. Right append sends a word $
u_1\cdots
u_n$ to $F_{
u_n}\cdots F_{
u_1}v_\Lambda$, whereas the generic multiplication convention assigns $F_{
u_1}\cdots F_{
u_n}$; on actual divided word classes the map is therefore $\pi_\Lambda\circ*$. Their $A$-span is all generic $K_0$ by [F1], so this determines the map integrally, giving $\phi[q_\Lambda(P)]=\pi_\Lambda(*(\gamma^{-1}[P]))$; composing with the generic mirror twist of [F2], which supplies exactly $*$ and commutes with projective Hom duality, gives $\pi_\Lambda$ without star.

citations.quote:
Let $M$ be $U^-$ or $V(\lambda)$ with the balanced triple and lower global basis of [[def-lower-global-basis-of-a-quantum-highest-weight-module]], and let $M_A^\vee$, $L_0^\vee$, $L_\infty^\vee$, $G^{\mathrm{up}}$, $\psi^\vee$ and the upper Kashiwara operators be as in [[def-upper-global-basis-by-the-contravariant-form]]. Then, for every finite root or label component:

(i) the restricted graded integral dual $M_A^\vee$ has the actual $A$-basis $\{G^{\mathrm{up}}(b)\}$ dual to $\{G(b)\}$, and $B:(M_A^\vee)_\mu\times(M_A)_\mu\to A$ is perfect; globally the bases form direct sums, which is restricted graded perfectness and not an identification with the full product $\operatorname{Hom}_A(M_A,A)$;

(ii) the dual charts are $L_0^\vee=\bigoplus_bA_0G^{\mathrm{up}}(b)$ and $L_\infty^\vee=\bigoplus_bA_\infty G^{\mathrm{up}}(b)$, and their balanced $\mathbb Z$-intersection is $\bigoplus_b\mathbb ZG^{\mathrm{up}}(b)$, so the dual triple is integrally balanced; at $0$ one has $L_0^\vee=L_0$ and the residue of $G^{\mathrm{up}}(b)$ is $b$, while at infinity its residue is the dual of the lower infinity-chart class in the dual infinity chart;

(iii) the upper bar $\psi^\vee$ fixes each $G^{\mathrm{up}}(b)$;

(iv) the upper operators satisfy the exact mixed adjunctions $B(\tilde f_i^{\mathrm{low}}u,v)=B(u,\tilde e_i^{\mathrm{up}}v)$ and $B(\tilde e_i^{\mathrm{low}}u,v)=B(u,\tilde f_i^{\mathrm{up}}v)$; their reductions at $0$ equal the lower crystal operators and give the same crystal set $\mathcal B$;

(v) for a divided module string $F_i^{(n)}v$ of top rank-one weight $a$, with $d_{a,n}=q_i^{n(a-n)}\binom{a}{n}_i$, the upper operator $\tilde e_i^{\mathrm{up}}$ at position $n\ge1$ multiplies the preceding divided position by $d_{a,n}/d_{a,n-1}=q_i^{a-2n+1}[a-n+1]_i/[n]_i$, and $\tilde f_i^{\mathrm{up}}$ at position $n<a$ multiplies the next by the reciprocal $d_{a,n}/d_{a,n+1}$; for a half string with $e_i'v=0$, with $d_n=q_i^{-n(n-1)/2}/[n]_i!=1/[n]_{q_i^2}!$, the ratios are $1/[n]_{q_i^2}$ and $[n+1]_{q_i^2}$. All admissible ratios are units congruent to $1$ in $A_0$, so unweighted lower adjunction holds modulo $q$ but generally not exactly over $\mathbb Q(q)$. No equality of upper and lower integral bases, even in symmetric type, is implied.

Let $H=H_v(n)$ be the normalized type-A Hecke algebra over $A=\mathbb Z[v^{\pm1}]$ and let $a$ be the $A$-linear anti-involution with $a(v)=v$, $a(H_x)=H_{x^{-1}}$ (the reversal anti-involution of the Hecke page). Define the trace $\varepsilon:H\to A$ by $\varepsilon(H_w)=\delta_{e,w}$ on the standard basis, and the bilinear form $(-,-):H\times H\to A$ by $(h,h'):=\varepsilon(a(h)h')$. Then:

1. $\varepsilon$ and $(-,-)$ are well defined (the $H_w$ are an $A$-basis);
2. $(ph,qh')=pq\,(h,h')$ for all $p,q\in A$ and all $h,h'$;
3. $(hH_s,h')=(h,h'H_s)$ and $(H_sh,h')=(h,H_sh')$ for all $h,h'$ and every simple reflection $s$;
4. the standard basis is orthonormal, $(H_x,H_y)=\delta_{xy}$;
5. on the Kazhdan-Lusztig basis, $(\underline H_x,\underline H_y)\in v\mathbb Z[v]$ for $x\ne y$ and $(\underline H_x,\underline H_x)\in 1+v\mathbb Z[v]$;
6. the form of (4) is the standard pairing $\langle H_x,H_y\rangle=\delta_{xy}$ used in the type-A Soergel Hom formula, transported through the identification of the normalized standard bases.

All statements are integral and use no choice.

