Independence. For a reduced word $w=s_{i_1}\cdots s_{i_m}$ the expansion of $A_w=A_{i_1}\cdots A_{i_m}$ in $Ktimes S_d$ has coefficient $c_w=\prod_{k=1}^{m}\Bigl(1+ rac1{(s_{i_1}\cdots s_{i_{k-1}})(\delta_{i_k})}\Bigr)$ at the permutation $w$, a product of nonzero rational functions; every other term uses a proper subword of a reduced expression, hence has strictly smaller permutation length by [F2]. Now suppose $\sum_{a,w}p_{a,w}(z)A_w=0$ is a finite relation with $p_{a,w}\in K$; choose a permutation $w$ of maximal length with some $p_{a,w}
e0$ and, among the finitely many exponents with that $w$, one with $p_{a,w}
e0$. The coefficient of $w$ in the crossed-product expansion is $p_{a,w}c_w
e0$, so the relation is nontrivial unless no term was present in the first place. To justify that a zero operator has zero crossed-product coefficients, use the finite independence of the distinct field automorphisms: if $\sum_{j}f_j\sigma_j=0$ with a minimal positive number of nonzero terms, choose $\sigma_1$ and any $t\in K$, and subtract from the composition of the relation with multiplication by $t$ the relation multiplied by $\sigma_1(t)$; the result $\sum_{j
e1}f_j(\sigma_j(t)-\sigma_1(t))\sigma_j=0$ is a shorter relation, nontrivial for a suitable $t$ at which some $\sigma_j$ differs from $\sigma_1$, and a one-term relation cannot annihilate $1$. Hence the coefficient argument is valid and the ordered monomials are $K$-linearly independent in the model; since the presentation relations are $P$-linear, the same triangular argument over $P$ gives their linear independence in $H_d^{\deg}$. [F2, step 2.1, algebra]

Independence by finite-field specialization. Suppose $\sum_b f_bb=0$ is a nontrivial relation in $H_n$ with $f_b\in R$, not all zero. Fix $b_0$ with $f:=f_{b_0}
e0$ and multiply the relation by a Laurent monomial so that $f\in\mathbb Z[z,U_1,\dots,U_\ell]$; choose a prime $p$ for which some nonzero integer coefficient of $f$ survives modulo $p$, and view $f$ as a polynomial in $U_1,\dots,U_\ell$ with coefficients in $\mathbb F_p[z]$; let $g(z)
e0$ be one of those coefficients. By [F3] the number of primitive elements of $\mathbb F_{p^d}^	imes$ is $\varphi(p^d-1)$, which is unbounded and satisfies $\varphi(N)\ge\sqrt{N/2}$ (factor $N$ over prime powers: each factor $\varphi(r^a)^2/r^a=r^{a-2}(r-1)^2\ge1$ except $r=2$, $a=1$, where it is $1/2$). Choose $d$ with $\varphi(p^d-1)>\deg g$ and $p^d-1$ larger than every $U$-degree of $f$; then some primitive $z\in\mathbb F_{p^d}^	imes$ avoids the roots of $g$. Repeated one-variable root bounds on the nonzero-coordinate grid (each $U_a$ is constrained to be a power $z^{c_a}
e0$, and the remaining polynomial in the next variable has degree bounded by the $U$-degree of $f$) supply $U_a=z^{c_a}
e0$ with $f(z,U_1,\dots,U_\ell)
e0$ in $\mathbb F_{p^d}$. This specialization is exactly an allowed finite cyclic type-A field parameter: $z$ has order $e=p^d-1\ge2$, and the cyclotomic charges $c_a$ lie in $\mathbb Z/e\mathbb Z$. [F1, F3, algebra]

Nonzero decomposition numbers force a $J$-chain. Put $d_{\lambda\mu}=[\Delta(\lambda):L(\mu)]$. For $\lambda
e\mu$, the radical $\Delta(\lambda)^1=\operatorname{rad}\Delta(\lambda)$ contains every composition factor $L(\mu)$ of $\Delta(\lambda)$, and all higher filtration terms lie in that radical, so the coefficient of $[L(\mu)]$ in the left side of the Jantzen identity is nonzero exactly when $d_{\lambda\mu}
e0$; on the right it is $\sum_
u J_{\lambda
u}d_{
u\mu}$. If $d_{\lambda\mu}
e0$, this integer is nonzero, so some $
u$ satisfies $J_{\lambda
u}
e0$ and $d_{
u\mu}
e0$, with $\lambdahd
u\unrhd\mu$. Repeating strictly decreases the index in the finite dominance poset until $
u=\mu$, so $\lambda\sim_J\mu$. Consequently any two standards sharing a simple factor are both $J$-connected to that factor's index, and therefore to each other. [F1, F2, F3, step 1.1]

